WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Secure backup scripting is a repeatable engineering discipline.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.600
Start with recovery goals.

3
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:13.020
Know what must be recovered, why it matters, how current the data must be, and how quickly restoration must happen.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:21.360
Classify the data so protection, access, storage, retention, and handling decisions match the sensitivity of the backup.

5
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:29.940
Include dependencies needed for real recovery, and exclude unnecessary data that only increases exposure and cost.

6
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.820
Script defensively.

7
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:44.960
Quote variables, validate source and destination paths, check prerequisites, handle command and pipeline failures, use safe temporary files, and fail clearly before partial changes become worse.

8
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.980
Avoid ambiguous cleanup behavior.

9
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:55.000
Make scripts understandable enough that another engineer can review what data is collected, where it goes, and what the script does when something unexpected happens.

10
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.160
Protect credentials, storage, transfers, and keys.

11
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.620
Use dedicated identities and least privilege.

12
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.640
Separate read, write, delete, and administrative permissions.

13
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.020
Keep secrets out of scripts, logs, and command history.

14
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:18.560
Use encryption at rest and in transit where appropriate, and keep encryption keys protected separately from backup data.

15
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.620
Treat backup destinations as security-critical systems because they often contain aggregated sensitive information.

16
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.500
Finally, prove that backups can support recovery.

17
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.720
Verify completion, record integrity evidence where practical, and test restores on a schedule that matches the importance of the system.

18
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:44.900
Manage retention safely, protect the last known-good backup, and consider versioning or immutability where appropriate.

19
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.280
Monitor every run, alert on failures and unusual behavior, review backup status, and improve legacy scripts over time.

20
00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:58.060
Backups are not merely an operational convenience; they are a security and resilience control.
